= Lecture 1, 6 January 2026
== So what is Topology about?
Topology is not about open spaces, it is about continuous functions. Just like linear algebra is not about vectors and vector spaces but about linear transforms.

#definition(title: "Topology on a Set X")[
  Topology on a set $X$ is a subset of $P(X)$ closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.
]
#remark[
  Let ${S_alpha | alpha in J}$ be an indexed family of subsets of $X$. Then
  $
  union.big_(x in J) S_alpha = {x in X | exists alpha in J "s.t." x in S_alpha}\
  inter.big_(x in J) S_alpha = {x in X | forall alpha in J, x in S_alpha}
  $

  This implies every topology on $X$ contains $X$ and $emptyset$.
]
We normally denote a topological space as $(X, tau)$ but we ignore the $tau$ for most cases other than some counter-examples.#footnote["We will not need to write this unless dealing with some of the ... wrong headed examples. This book (Munkers) abhors in such examples."]

We will study more about geometric topology and glueing etc.
#example[
- For any $X$, discrete topology refers to $tau = P(X)$ while indiscrete topology refers to $tau = {emptyset, P(X)}$.
- Finite Complement Topology#footnote[Will come in as the Zariski Topology for the affine line, words I don't know the meaning of]
- Countable Complement Topology
- The usual topologies of $RR, RR^n$, any metric spaces.
]
#definition(title: "Basis for a Topology")[
  The basis for a topology on $X$ is a subset $cal(B)$ of $P(X)$ such that
  $
  union.big_(v in cal(B)) v = X
  $
  Also, given $x in B_1 inter B_2, B_i in cal(B)$:
  $
  exists B_3 in cal(B) "s.t." x in B_3 subset B_1 inter B_2
  $
  Given an abstract basis $B$ in $P(X)$, the topology generated by $B$ consists of those sets $U$ such that $forall x in U, exists B in cal(B) "s.t." x in B subset.eq U$.
]
#rmk[
  This gives a topology. Also, note: each basis element is open.
]
#thm[
  $U$ is open in this topology $<==>$ $U$ is union of basis elements
]
#proof[
  #todo[?]
]
#definition(title : "Subbasis")[
  An abstract subbasis is any subset $cal(S)$ of $P(X)$ such that
  $
  union.big_(v in cal(S)) v = X
  $
  then the associated basis is set of all finite intersections of elements of $cal(S)$.
]

Suppose I am separately given $tau$, a topology on $X$ and $cal(B)$ is an abstract basis in $P(X)$. We say $cal(B)$ generates $tau$ $<==>$ if each $B in cal(B)$ is in $Tau$ and for any $U in Tau$ is union of elements in $B$.
#definition[
If $tau_1, tau_2$ are two topologies on $X$, we say $tau_2$ is finer than $tau_1$ and $tau_1$ is corser than $tau_2$ if $tau_1 subset.eq tau_2$.
]

== $(X, d)$ metric space
Take $cal(B) = "open balls", B_epsilon(a) = {x in X | d(x,a) < epsilon}$. This is a basis by triangle inequality. 
#example[
  - $RR$ has basis $(a,b)$
  - $RR^2$ has basis balls ($l_2$) as well as diamonds ($l_1$) (we have already seen that all norms are equivalent on finite dimensional $RR^n$) 
]

