== Continuity
#defn[
  $X -->^f Y$ is continuous\
  $<==>$ $f^(-1)("open in" Y)$ is open in $X$\
  $<==>$ $f^(-1)("closed in" Y)$ is closed in $X$\
  $<==>$ $f(overline(A)) subset overline(f(A))$\
  $<==>$ $f$ is continuous at each $x in X$
]
#rmk[
  To determine if $f$ is cont, it is enough to check $f^(-1)("basis elements")$ is open.
]
#cor[
  $X -->^f Y "is continuous" ==> X_"finer" -->^f Y "is continuous"$
]
#example[
  Following are continuous:
  - Any $X$, $X -->^id X$
  - $X -->^f Y -->^g Z$ where $f, g$ is continuous then $g compose f$ is continuous.
  - any $X -->^"constant" "any" Y$
  - Inclusion of any subspace
  - restriction of domain
  - extend codomain or restrict codomain if possible
  - local formulation of continuity. If ${V_alpha}_(alpha in I)$ is an open cover of $X$ then $X -->^f Y$ is continuous $<==>$ $f|_(V_alpha) : V_alpha --> Y$ is continuous $forall alpha$
  - Pasting Lemma for finitely many closed sets
]
== Product Topology
Let's say we somehow have
$
RR times RR -->^(+, dot) RR\ 
RR -->^(-) RR\ 
RR backslash {0} -->^(1/x) RR\ 
$
are continuous. This gives as many more. Say we want to show $x^2$ is continuous
$
&RR -->^Delta &RR times RR --> RR\
&x |-> &(x,x)\
& &(x,y) |-> &x y
$

Basically, we want that if $f, g: RR --> RR$ are continuous then $f + g : RR --> RR$ is continuous. We can do that via the continuity of $f times g : RR --> RR times RR$.

Thus us what we want our product topology to achieve.
#claim[
  If $X -->^(f_i) Y_i $ is continuous then $X -->^(f_1 times f_2) Y_1 times Y_2$ is continuous.
]

Let's try using $V_1 times V_2$ where $V_i$ is open in $Y_i$ should be open in $Y_1 times Y_2$.
$
(V_1 times V_2) inter (W_1 times W_2) = (V_1 inter W_1) times (V_2 inter W_2)
$
but
$
(V_1 times V_2) union (W_1 times W_2) != (V_1 union W_1) times (V_2 union W_2)
$
To get the latter to be true, we define it as
#definition[
  $Y_1 times Y_2$ becomes a topological space by treating open $times$ open is a basis.
]

Furthermore, observe that $Y_1 times Y_2 -->^p_i Y_i $ being the projection maps are continuous as:
$
p_1^(-1)(V_1) = V_1 times Y_2\
p_2^(-1)(V_2) = Y_1 times V_2\
p_1^(-1)(V_1) inter p_2^(-1)(V_2) = V_1 times V_2
$

This implies that the product topology is the coarsest topology on $Y_1 times Y_2$ that makes projections continuous.

#claim[
  $(Y_1 times Y_2, p_1, p_2)$ is the universal topological space with continuous maps to $Y_1$ and $Y_2$.
]
This means, given continuous $Z ->^(f_i) Y_i$ then $exists ! f : Z -> Y_1 times Y_2$ such that
$
f_i = p_i compose f
$
