== Isomorphism of Topological Spaces
Notice, Isomorphism is just renaming while preserving structure.

#defn(title:"Bad Hopeful Definition")[
  A bijective continuous map
]
Why is this bad? Consider $(X, tau)$ then
$
(X, tau) -->^id (X, tau')  
$
is continuous and bijective if $tau$ is finer. This has by no means preserved structure!

#definition(title:"Isomorphism")[
  An isomorphism is a map
  $
  X -->^f Y "s.t."\
  exists X <--^g Y "with"\
  f g = id_Y, g f = id_X\
  $
  (Such $g_1$ if it exists is unique: $g_2 = (g_1 f) g_2 = g_1$)
]
#defn[
  A continuous map $f : X --> Y$ is called a homeomorphism if exists a continuous map $g : Y --> X$ such that $f g = id_Y, g f = id_X$
]